{
 "schema": 1,
 "note": "Output parsers of the dead-code engines: DATA read by graph/adapters.py. Every format has a recording of the real engine in tests/fixtures/engines/<tool>/<version>/ and a contract test; a field no recording shows is not mapped (knip: files and exports only). Claims for rule ids that no recording shows come from the engine documentation (unverified). A field path: name | name[] | name[key] (items whose key is truthy) | [] ; '.' joins steps, '' is the document, '=text' a constant, 'a|b' the first non-empty, a leading '^' reads the outer row (rows/each); xml: @attr, child/@attr, child, '.'. A json format states the shape it expects (top: list|object, need: keys of an object, fail: a list that must be empty, e.g. tflint errors[]) and an xml format its root element, a text format the line of an engine error (fail_re, e.g. tsc error TS5058 without a file): output of another shape is an AdapterError, never an empty list. ok_rc: the exit codes of a healthy run (0 and the findings code, as the recordings show; tsc 2 is from the tsc documentation, unverified); an engine without ok_rc has an exit code that counts something or that no recording pins down, so it is not checked. claims: [rule regex, claim kind | {message regex: claim kind}]; claim_nodes: the graph node kinds a claim may be about; no_dispute: claims that are evidence only.",
 "claim_nodes": {
  "func": [
   "func",
   "method"
  ],
  "method": [
   "method",
   "func"
  ],
  "class": [
   "class",
   "iface"
  ],
  "var": [
   "var",
   "const"
  ],
  "const": [
